# keur security control — non-literal command into keur's OWN shell sink.
#
# Why this exists (and why it is separate from KEUR-SEC-005):
#   KEUR-SEC-005 guards the libc shell sinks (system()/popen()). But keur does
#   not call those directly in business code — it routes every external command
#   through its process adapter:
#       Proc::run(const std::string& cmd)      -> platform::run(cmd)
#       Proc::capture(const std::string& cmd)  -> platform::capture(cmd)
#   and platform::run / platform::capture / platform::capture_bytes are the
#   actual shell sinks underneath. That is a keur-specific sink class, so it
#   gets a keur-specific control instead of overloading SEC-005's context-free
#   string-concat heuristic (which mis-fired on non-exec std::cout builders).
#
# What it flags:
#   A call to one of keur's shell sinks — .run(...) / .capture(...) on a Proc,
#   or platform::run/capture/capture_bytes(...) — whose FIRST argument is NOT a
#   bare "..." string literal (i.e. a variable, a concatenation, or *.c_str()).
#   A constructed command may carry shell metacharacters (CWE-78).
#
# What it does NOT flag:
#   A sink called with a fixed string literal (run("git --version")). Those are
#   safe by construction and allowed.
#
# The fix is shell-free execution: give the adapter an argv-vector primitive
# (Proc::run_argv -> platform::run_argv via posix_spawn/CreateProcess, no
# /bin/sh) and pass arguments as a vector, never as an interpolated shell
# string. This mirrors the tool_on_path fix (KEUR-SEC-005) and the
# no-posix-shell-in-exec line (KEUR-PORT-005).
#
# @see rules/security/KEUR-SEC-005 (libc sibling: system/popen), ADR-003
#      (adapter layer), ADR-005 (platform layer), KEUR-PORT-005.
id: KEUR-SEC-006
title: Non-literal command passed to keur's shell adapter/platform sink
category: cmdi
severity: critical
engine: pattern
action: block
skip_comments: true
target:
  extensions: .c .cpp .cc
  # Exclude the sink layer itself: common/ (platform::run/capture definitions)
  # and adapters/ (the Proc pass-through) are the single, documented place where
  # a shell string legitimately lives — same convention KEUR-PORT-* uses for
  # common/. Business code (cmd/, learn/, report/) must stay shell-free.
  exclude_paths: vendor/ ../rules/ _test.cpp test/ tests/ build/ dist/ common/ adapters/
patterns:
  # A keur shell sink (.run/.capture on a Proc, or platform::run/capture/
  # capture_bytes) whose first argument is not a bare "..." literal. `exclude`
  # (pattern-not, ADR-007) drops the safe cases:
  #   1. the whole argument is a single complete "..." literal closed by ')';
  #   2. the receiver is the Git adapter (git.run / git.capture) — that method
  #      is shell-free by construction (it splits args and spawns git via an
  #      argv vector through platform::capture_argv, no /bin/sh), so a variable
  #      passed to it cannot reach a shell. @see adapters/git.cpp, KEUR-SEC-006.
  # A concatenation that merely starts with a literal (run("'" + home + "'"))
  # is NOT excluded — it still carries interpolated input into a shell sink.
  - regex: \b(?:platform::(?:run|capture|capture_bytes)|(?:\w+\.)?(?:run|capture))\s*\(
    exclude: (?:\bgit\.(?:run|capture)\s*\(|\b(?:platform::(?:run|capture|capture_bytes)|(?:\w+\.)?(?:run|capture))\s*\(\s*"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"\s*\))
    message: "Non-literal command passed to a keur shell sink (Proc::run/.capture or platform::run/capture) — the command was constructed and may carry shell metacharacters (CWE-78). Assemble arguments as an argv vector (Proc::run_argv -> platform::run_argv, posix_spawn/CreateProcess, no /bin/sh), not an interpolated shell string. Reserve the string form for fixed literals. @see KEUR-SEC-006, ADR-003, ADR-005"
confidence: 0.85
fix: "Replace the interpolated shell string with a shell-free argv call: add an argv-vector primitive to the process adapter (Proc::run_argv routing to platform::run_argv, implemented with posix_spawn / CreateProcess and no shell) and pass the command as a vector of arguments. Reserve Proc::run/.capture(string) for fixed literal commands with zero interpolated input. See ADR-003, ADR-005, and KEUR-SEC-006."
